Template-Type: ReDIF-Article 1.0
Author-Name: Akanksha Aravinda Gowda
Title: Methods Used to Study Bitcoin Price Behaviour, 2000-2026: A Review and Directions for Future Research
Abstract: While the Bitcoin study has rapidly grown, there has been relatively little study of the dominant methods applied to Bitcoin research and the methods that are underused and deserve more research attention. This note outlines the techniques used in analysing Bitcoin price, returns and volatility. 127 records were identified from Google Scholar for the time span 2000-2026, which were kept for 110 studies after screening. A quantitative design was used in more studies (102 studies; 92.7%) compared to qualitative (5 studies; 4.5%) and mixed designs (3 studies; 2.7%). The largest method family was machine learning forecasting. The note highlights that methods employed in qualitative social research are relevant to Bitcoin, given that its price is driven by investor beliefs, narratives and regulatory developments that statistical modeling cannot adequately capture.
Journal: Global Confluence Review
Volume: 1
Issue: 3
Year: 2026
